  "name": "Leo kiʻekiʻe (Hawaiian falsetto and hula kuʻi song)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Hawaiian-language song style for hula kuʻi and later hula ʻauana, developed from the late nineteenth century when Hawaiian poetry was set to Western-influenced melodies with guitar and ukulele, and marked by leo kiʻekiʻe, a falsetto that leaps across the haʻi, the break between chest and head voice. Genoa Keawe and Bill Aliʻiloa Lincoln were celebrated falsetto singers, and the Aloha Festivals falsetto contest continues the style.",
  "recipe": "classic North American mid-1950s leo kiʻekiʻe, functional Song, tropical song branch. reinforced falsetto, solo with instrumental accompaniment. traditional koa ukulele, parlor acoustic guitar, open e hawaiian slack lap steel, upright bass. honolulu hawaiian slack key lanai. passive ribbon, tube pre, German tube console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. tape 15ips.",
